A||N||7||Black88GT||4 core radiator||||5||
Z||000000||Black88GT||07-12-2000||10:54 AM||||I was wondering if anyone knows where to buy these for Fiero's.  Could someone explain why a 4 core is better then a 2 core? I have no idea. ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||07-12-2000||10:59 AM||||4-core cools more volume of water. Hence the entire system runs cooler. The only place I know that sells one is V-8 Archie. His 4-core is a drop-in replacement. I don't know what his cost is. <BR>His website is: <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com" TARGET=_blank>www.v8archie.com</A>  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Black88GT||07-12-2000||11:04 AM||||I need a new radiator thats why I was asking.  I couldn't find it on his website, guess I will have to email him.  Would it be worth it to get a 4 core over a 2 core.||||reg||5
Z||000003||lennyb4||07-12-2000||11:13 AM||||Unless you have made extensive modifications the cost of a four core would not be worth it (probally double the cost then a two core). A V8 would require a four core due to the increased engine size and cooling abilities & requirements, where as with a 4 or 6 cyl. it would just be over kill. I would just buy a replacement 2 core and use the money for some other mods....<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smallPegasus.gif">  <BR>87 Coupe... Got rice???||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||07-12-2000||12:13 PM||||The bigger radiator won't solve the root problem that Fiero cooling has. That is an issue of front end airflow. A 4" radiator will just show the problem more slowly. You still have to adress either a lower temp fan control or(and) front end ventilation by adding hood vents. I would do either of those before adding a bigger radiator which can actually cause problems with a 4 or V6.||||reg||1
Z||000005||coyote||07-12-2000||02:00 PM||||as far as the swap goes it is very easy, however it will most likely make the car run to cool, it will never reach optimum temp. As theogre has stated the hood vents are the way to go, i do know that the 4 core radiator is borderline in a very hot enviroment and typicallly the hood is vented to promote air flow.  It may be that a 4 core with no vents might work, but for the price i would get the hood vented.<P>Coyote<BR>84 SE<BR>87 GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||07-12-2000||09:20 PM||||I have a 4 core radiator too, I highly recommend getting one with a V8. The temperature here has been mid to upper 90's for over a week and I've had no overheating problems. I've also been diving my V8 Batmobile every day since my 85 GT broke down (about 100 miles per day). It runs right at 180 degrees all the time. I have the cooling fan coming on at 160 degrees and I have a 180 degree thermostat. A 4 core is probably overkill for a stock V6.||||reg||1
Z||000007||V8Fiero||07-12-2000||09:50 PM||||I have done the V8 swap and am now in the process of looking for a four core radiator myself. I just cant part with the $350 that archie wants for his radiator. I was wondering if anyone has tried with any success, a radiator from say, JEGS, or Summit?<BR>Thanks.<BR>||||reg||1
